- How to optimize min queries? N log N via RMQ, or a deque per i.
- How to reconstruct the exact subarrays efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP formulation for selecting fixed-count non-overlapping subarrays with a per-block cost, plus reconstruction of blocks.

## Key Concepts

- DP over i (prefix) and t (blocks)
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- Running best to avoid O(n^2)
- Reconstructing start/end indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarraysWithCost(A, k, c){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const from = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  const bestVal = new Array(k+1).fill(NEG);
  const bestIdx = new Array(k+1).fill(-1);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++){
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      from[i][t] = {type:'skip', at:i-1, t};
    }
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++){
      const val = dp[i-1][t-1] - pref[i-1];
      if (val > bestVal[t]) { bestVal[t] = val; bestIdx[t] = i-1; }
      const cand = bestVal[t] + pref[i] - c;
      if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = cand;
        from[i][t] = {type:'end', end:i-1, start:bestIdx[t], t};
      }
    }
  }
  let i = n, t = k;
  const blocks = [];
  while (i > 0 && t > 0){
    const info = from[i][t];
    if (!info) break;
    if (info.type === 'end'){
      blocks.push([info.start, info.end]);
      i = info.start;
      t--;
    } else {
      i = info.at;
    }
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for at-most-k blocks?
- How would you handle negative values of c or allow zero-cost blocks?
- What modifications optimize space to O(k) while preserving reconstruction?

## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem evaluates proficiency in tree DP with disjoint-path packing and exact-path-count constraints, along with path reconstruction. It combines knapsack-on-tree techniques with path endpoint handling while guaranteeing vertex-disjointness.

## Key Concepts

- Tree DP with multiple states per node (unused vs. endpoint vs. interior)
- Knapsack-style merging across children via DP table convolution
- Path reconstruction through stored decision pointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function dfs(u, parent) {
    const dp0 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), dp1 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), dp2 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF);
    dp0[u][0] = dp1[u][1] = 0;
    for (const v of children[u]) {
        dfs(v, u);
        const ndp0 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), ndp1 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), ndp2 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF);
        for (let i = 0; i <= k; i++) for (let j = 0; j + i <= k; j++) {
            ndp0[i + j] = Math.max(ndp0[i + j], dp0[u][i] + max(dp0[v][j], dp1[v][j], dp2[v][j]));
            ndp1[i + j] = Math.max(ndp1[i + j], dp1[u][i] + max(dp0[v][j], dp2[v][j]));
            ndp2[i + j] = Math.max(ndp2[i + j], dp2[u][i] + dp0[v][j]);
        }
        dp0 = ndp0; dp1 = ndp1; dp2 = ndp2;
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests understanding of dynamic programming with non-overlapping segments where the segment value has a quadratic structure. It challenges candidates to handle state transitions, reconstruction, and complexity analysis systematically.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix arrays with block count tracking
- Transition by considering all possible block endpoints
- Prefix sum optimization for O(1) subarray sum queries
- Backpointers for optimal solution reconstruction
- Time-space trade-off analysis

## Code Implementation
```javascript
function maxSubarrayValue(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const prefix = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + A[i];
  }

  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prevStart = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // Skip i
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
        prevStart[i][t] = prevStart[i-1][t];
      }

      // End block at i
      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const blockValue = (prefix[i] - prefix[j-1]) * (i-j+1);
        const candidate = dp[j-1][t-1] + blockValue;
        if (candidate > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          prevStart[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction
  const result = [];
  let currI = n, currT = k;
  while (currT > 0 && currI > 0) {
    const start = prevStart[currI][currT];
    if (start !== -1 && start !== prevStart[currI-1][currT]) {
      result.unshift([start-1, currI-1]);
      currI = start - 1;
      currT--;
    } else {
      currI--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], subarrays: result };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) if all array elements are non-negative?
- What techniques could reduce space complexity to O(n) while maintaining reconstruction?
- How does the solution change if subarrays can overlap?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design a constrained dynamic programming solution for block partitioning problems with specific boundary conditions.

## Key Concepts

- Block partitioning with exact count and minimum length constraints
- Prefix sums for O(1) block value computation
- 2D DP with reconstruction pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and optimization opportunities

## Code Example

```javascript
// DP recurrence for blocks with minimum length L
for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    dp[i][t] = -INF;
    for (let s = L; s <= i; s++) {
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i-s][t-1] + (prefix[i] - prefix[i-s]));
    }
  }
}
```

## Implementation Considerations

- Time complexity: O(n × k × (n-L)) = O(n²k)
- Space: O(n × k) for DP table plus O(n) for prefix sums
- Add parent pointer array for reconstruction
- Base cases: dp[i][0] = 0, dp[0][t>0] = -∞

## Why This Is Asked
This problem introduces a dynamic programming angle where subarrays are weighted by their block index. It tests the ability to transform interval sums into prefix sums, derive a two-dimensional DP over position and block count, and implement solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for O(1) interval sum computation
- 2D DP over position and block count
- Solution reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time complexity: O(n²k); Space complexity: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScorePartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = [0];
  for (let x of A) P.push(P[P.length-1] + x);
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][1] = P[i];
  }

  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t-1; p < i; p++) {
        const score = dp[p][t-1] + (P[i] - P[p]) * t;
        if (score > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = score;
          parent[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct partitions
  const partitions = [];
  let end = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    const start = parent[end][t];
    partitions.unshift([start+1, end]);
    end = start;
  }

  return { maxScore: dp[n][k], partitions };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP on subsequences with transition costs; requires deriving a 2D DP with O(n^2 m) time and backpointers. Edge cases: m=1 yields 0. Consider tie-breaking on reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on subsequences; 2D states; O(n^2 m) time; backtracking via parents; rolling arrays for space.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSubseq(A, m){
  const n = A.length;
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, ()=>Array(m+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, ()=>Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0; i<n; i++) dp[i][1] = 0;
  for(let i=0; i<n; i++){
    for(let t=2; t<=m; t++){
      for(let p=0; p<i; p++){
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + Math.abs(A[i]-A[p]);
        if(val < dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = INF, end = -1;
  for(let i=0; i<n; i++){ if(dp[i][m] < best){ best = dp[i][m]; end = i; } }
  const idx = []; let t=m, cur = end;
  while(t >= 1){ idx.push(cur); cur = par[cur][t]; t--; }
  idx.reverse();
  return {cost: best, indices: idx};
}
```

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This problem tests your ability to design multi-dimensional DP by combining deletion constraints with pairwise scoring across adjacent characters. It evaluates state space design, transition logic, and reconstruction from a full solution path.

## Key Concepts
- **State design**: A 3D DP tracks position i (0 to n), deletions used d (0 to k), and the last kept character L (0–25, or 26 for none).
- **Transition logic**: The delete transition preserves state; the keep transition accumulates score only when a previous character exists, using W[prev][cur].
- **Reconstruction**: Backtracking from the optimal endpoint to determine which characters were deleted.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScore(s, W, k) {
    const n = s.length, NONE = 26;
    const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () =>
        Array.from({length: k+1}, () =>
            Array(NONE+1).fill(-Infinity)));
    dp[0][0][NONE] = 0;

    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
            for (let L = 0; L <= NONE; L++) {
                if (dp[i][d][L] === -Infinity) continue;
                // Delete
                if (d < k) dp[i+1][d+1][L] = Math.max(dp[i+1][d+1][L], dp[i][d][L]);
                // Keep
                const cur = s.charCodeAt(i) - 97;
                const gain = L === NONE ? 0 : W[L][cur];
                dp[i+1][d][cur] = Math.max(dp[i+1][d][cur], dp[i][d][L] + gain);
            }
        }
    }
    // Find best among d ≤ k
    let best = 0, bestD = 0, bestL = NONE;
    for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++)
        for (let L = 0; L <= NONE; L++)
            if (dp[n][d][L] > best) { best = dp[n][d][L]; bestD = d; bestL = L; }
    return { score: best, deletions: bestD, lastKept: bestL };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- **Memory optimization**: Can you reduce space to O(27·k) using rolling arrays?
- **Negative weights**: How would you handle cases where W contains negative values—would your approach change?

